I do tend to subscribe to the idea of  not having or focusing on regrets  because everything we've done sort of  makes us who we are now and I'm happy  with who I am now and especially in  terms of my academic pathway and my  career in physics but if I was to look  back at my undergrad degree in hindsight  there are probably things I would try to  do differently I don't know how they  would affect where I do end up maybe it  would be better maybe it would be worse  but there are probably things I would  try to change so one of them is probably  trying to do more courses in or to do a  minor in computer science so I've spoken  about the importance of coding and  computer science  in terms of physics before but I do  think it is really important and  becoming more so I took some courses in  computer science but not enough for a  minor or a major I instead did my majors  in physics and math it's hard to say  with this one because to have done a  major or a minor in the computer science  as well like I was already taking more  courses than you're supposed to every  semester so I would have had to cut  something out maybe it cut the maths out  and I probably could have done without  the maths since I'm doing an  experimental physics project for my PhD  but I enjoyed the maths all the same in  some ideal situation I would have had  enough room to do the triple major  without adding any more time on to my  degree like another year or another  semester because I didn't want to do  that but yeah I think ideally I would  have done some more of those Comp Sci  courses the second thing I wanted to  talk about was kind of my approach to  studying at University  and it came down to essentially being a  memorization and cramming task for every  exam period at the time this felt like  the best way to get through because we  had so many assignments and so many  courses that I always found myself sort  of disregarding that some of the really  deep connections and and meanings behind  what we were doing and just focused on  how do I get the best grade in the exam  and that really became my focus  especially in exam period and I don't  like that especially in hindsight I look  back and I think I really wish I  actually understood that concept more  even though I got a good grade on it I  don't know like with this it seems to be  almost a systemic thing sort of across  people and across courses that the Uni  system is focused more on outcomes and  grades than it is on your deeper  understanding so I don't know if this  was entirely my fault or just the way  kind of things have to be if you want to  get through it's it's also hard to look  back and say I would change that  approach because I did get good grades  throughout undergrad and if I'd taken  time to really slow down and really  learn I don't know if I would have had  time to do everything that I needed to  do so it's a bit of both but definitely  I look back and I think I probably wish  that I asked more questions more  questions of my friends but also of the  lecturers and enter really trying to  understand deeply the content rather  than being happy with just getting a  good grade on it I think the most common  questions that I ask my friends are just  hey how do you do  question 3 on the assignment  I think now that I'm a PhD student I  have more time to investigate those  deeper learnings and so that's something  I'm trying to do now because in  hindsight I didn't really get the time  to appreciate it as an undergrad I hope  that that helps someone out there and  thanks for watching
 

